00:32:00.570 --> 00:32:07.580
Steve Thomas. Welcome to New Orleans, the Big Easy, the Crescent City, the city that care forgot.

00:32:08.420 --> 00:32:13.250
Now before I take you over to our This Old House project, let's go to the Old Quarter and see what

00:32:13.250 --> 00:32:17.470
some consider one of the finest examples of high Victorian architecture in the city,

00:32:17.710 --> 00:32:26.350
the Gallier House. Now in New Orleans, the French Quarter, or Via Carrée, is where all the tours

00:32:26.350 --> 00:32:29.050
begin and end. And for good reason.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:36.360
Some of the most extraordinary houses in the world this one for example the Gallier house is actually a museum

00:32:36.360 --> 00:32:40.440
But we're here to talk to the museum's director and miss all

00:32:51.120 --> 00:32:59.980
We've been expecting you thanks. Welcome to the gallier house. Look at this. I know I'm in New Orleans, but I feel like I'm walking into one of the grand mansions

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:04.930
of san francisco or even new york well victorian architecture had a lot of things in common across

00:33:04.930 --> 00:33:08.790
the country but in new orleans we have some special things that you'll be seeing well lead

00:33:08.790 --> 00:33:16.160
on good this is the double parlors of the house aren't they grand yeah this is not only where

00:33:16.160 --> 00:33:20.860
they entertained but we think the gallier probably enjoyed showing off his skills as an architect in

00:33:20.860 --> 00:33:26.650
this room and the table we have it set up right now as though it were new year's day the galliars

00:33:26.650 --> 00:33:29.750
are expecting some company to come, and this was the day that they exchanged.

00:33:30.120 --> 00:33:35.460
gifts and probably a lot of people went home with little cornucopias filled with sweets and

00:33:35.460 --> 00:33:41.520
sugared almonds. Oh, what a nice touch. And I can see Mr. Gallier enjoyed the odd Havana along with

00:33:41.520 --> 00:33:47.510
his evening newspaper. Yes, we think so. He also had 150 bottles of Claret wine in the cellar when

00:33:47.510 --> 00:33:52.950
he died in 1868. He's a man after my odd heart. Well, these are unusual, aren't they, these columns?

00:33:53.130 --> 00:33:57.410
Well, they are for New Orleans at this time. Most of the pollers were divided by sliding wooden

00:33:57.410 --> 00:33:59.810
doors, which of course made it easier to heat.

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:04.660
room but they were more concerned really about the summer heat and this would

00:34:04.660 --> 00:34:07.660
have facilitated the airflow and also make the rooms look a little more

00:34:07.660 --> 00:34:11.520
elegant. Now would these have been wood? Yes, they're made of carved wood as are the

00:34:11.520 --> 00:34:16.130
capitals but they've been gilded again to make them look even fancier. They are fancy

00:34:16.130 --> 00:34:20.210
indeed as are these cornice moldings and this rosette in the middle of the room

00:34:20.210 --> 00:34:24.430
here. These are all made of plaster and we were lucky they survived and they're

00:34:24.430 --> 00:34:29.890
painted their original blue color. Now what about the faces? Well in

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:35.220
In the 19th century, a lot of the cornices were stock cornices, the same elements over and over again.

00:34:35.620 --> 00:34:40.440
But Gallier designed this specifically for the room, and we don't know what those animals are.

00:34:40.520 --> 00:34:41.140
What do you think?

00:34:41.460 --> 00:34:45.090
Well, to tell you the truth, they look a little bit like Rottweilers to me.

00:34:45.630 --> 00:34:47.150
Well, I haven't heard that one before.

00:34:47.270 --> 00:34:50.430
They usually tell us lions and monkeys on the school group.

