Now, these drapes would be high Victorian, wouldn't they?

00:34:52.890 --> 00:34:53.350
Yes, indeed.

00:34:53.470 --> 00:34:58.330
These were copied from French drapery books of the time, and they're made of silk damask.

00:34:58.720 --> 00:34:59.980
And you can see they have some beautiful...

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:04.230
trim on them as well as these lovely tassels of which they were very fun.

00:35:04.390 --> 00:35:08.600
Well the colors just stand out, don't they? The colors at this time were nice, clear,

00:35:08.820 --> 00:35:12.430
brilliant colors. Now speaking of fabrics, what about the broad loom carpet?

00:35:12.920 --> 00:35:16.860
Well the carpets were woven in England on antique looms. The looms were

00:35:16.860 --> 00:35:21.200
27 inches wide and the strips came to the house and then

00:35:21.200 --> 00:35:27.830
were sewn together right on the side. Was this garden scene painted by a local painter?

00:35:28.010 --> 00:35:29.910
Oh no, in fact the wallpaper man

00:35:30.120 --> 00:35:35.920
manufacturers would be happy to hear that. This is an original wallpaper from 1852 and it won a

00:35:35.920 --> 00:35:41.380
price that year as the most illusionistic wallpaper. So you could actually order this

00:35:41.380 --> 00:35:59.400
wallpaper from Paris? Yes, you could. It took a little bit longer. Boy, look at this room. This is really something. And here's a more typical wallpaper printed from woodblocks, a more repetitive pattern and less illusionistic, of course. I know I'm supposed to be concentrating on the architecture, but this table is...

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:00.900
Sumptuous, indeed.

00:36:01.200 --> 00:36:02.100
Looks good, doesn't it?

00:36:02.220 --> 00:36:04.140
It sure does. I have a bit of a sweet tooth, so...

00:36:04.140 --> 00:36:08.300
Well, the galliars are entertaining at a dessert party one evening during the holidays.

00:36:08.730 --> 00:36:11.810
They love sweets and decorated foods at that time.

00:36:12.010 --> 00:36:14.490
Now, could you cut me a slice of this one right here?

00:36:14.530 --> 00:36:16.890
Well, I could cut you a slice, but it's two weeks old.

00:36:16.890 --> 00:36:18.290
It probably wouldn't taste like much.

00:36:18.430 --> 00:36:19.830
Well, maybe we'll try the Café du Monde.

00:36:19.930 --> 00:36:21.050
I'd recommend that.

00:36:22.090 --> 00:36:24.070
So this would have been a butler's pantry in here?

00:36:24.410 --> 00:36:28.150
Yes. It was used for the storage of the silver and crystal and fine china.

00:36:28.410 --> 00:36:29.970
Then the pots came up from the...

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:33.820
kitchen with the food on it and the food was transferred to beautiful china

00:36:34.340 --> 00:36:38.310
platters and such before it was served. Now where is the kitchen? It's right this

00:36:38.310 --> 00:36:45.950
way down the back hall. First we have a storeroom though and in here they would

00:36:45.950 --> 00:36:51.700
have had the sugar and beans, the wine, the staples for the household. On the

00:36:51.700 --> 00:36:56.550
counter you see a glass flycatcher. They would have put cobalt and sugar water in

00:36:56.550 --> 00:36:59.890
here. The flies would have gone in attracted by this and

00:37:00.640 --> 00:37:05.780
Bash themselves against the glass that they were trying to get out now. What's the contraption on the floor?

00:37:06.140 --> 00:37:07.220
It's a rat trap

00:37:07.220 --> 00:37:11.240
Looks more like a lobster trap big rats in New Orleans

00:37:12.190 --> 00:37:20.820
So this is the kitchen yes indeed. It's tiny isn't it especially when you consider that there were ten people living in the house and food

00:37:20.820 --> 00:37:29.970
Preparation was quite a bit more laborious than it is today. Well, I love the the ovens there, but what catches my eyes this big pierced

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:35.990
the copper vessel of some sort. Looks like lead pipes coming out the top and down along the wall

00:37:35.990 --> 00:37:41.020
to these spigots. Yes, well this was a great innovation. They had a complete plumbing system

00:37:41.020 --> 00:37:46.310
in the house and it all centered around the cast iron range where the water was heated. Of course

00:37:46.310 --> 00:37:51.340
the cooking was much easier on the range than it was open hearth and that enabled them to attach

00:37:51.340 --> 00:37:56.570
the kitchen to the rest of the house which was more convenient. But the water went from here

00:37:56.570 --> 00:37:59.790
into the comfort boiler where it was recirculated and heated.

00:38:00.480 --> 00:38:04.410
then drawn off in the lead pipes. They had no idea, of course, that the lead pipes were

00:38:04.410 --> 00:38:08.740
a health hazard. Now, where did the domestic water come from? I imagine this was before

00:38:08.740 --> 00:38:16.390
a municipal water system. Oh, well, I'll show you right out here. Okay. Well, look at this.

00:38:16.680 --> 00:38:21.310
This is unexpected, isn't it? Pretty, isn't it? All the French Quarter buildings had

00:38:21.310 --> 00:38:26.400
their backyards as courtyards. There were no front yards at this time. And Gallier designed

00:38:26.400 --> 00:38:29.760
this to be a decorative space with a Bermuda grass plot and a fountain.

00:38:30.280 --> 00:38:37.450
But it was also very utilitarian because the servants had to come out here to peel potatoes or wash clothes or whatever they needed to do.

00:38:38.050 --> 00:38:40.380
It's quiet, serene back here.

00:38:40.670 --> 00:38:44.330
Well, you asked about the water, though, and I'll show you where the cistern is. It's to the back.

00:38:44.570 --> 00:38:49.640
Okay. Another little courtyard back here.

00:38:49.910 --> 00:38:51.370
This is more the working courtyard.

00:38:51.570 --> 00:38:52.670
Yeah, this is fabulous.

00:38:53.030 --> 00:38:59.700
The water was collected off of the roof, went into the turned metal top that you see. It's quite decorative.

00:39:00.250 --> 00:39:03.750
And that tops a 5,000-gallon cypress cistern.

00:39:03.990 --> 00:39:05.750
And this was where the water was stored.

00:39:06.110 --> 00:39:09.350
The cistern is raised on this brick base for two reasons.

00:39:09.840 --> 00:39:12.300
One, it kept the wood away from the rot and the turmoil.

00:39:12.800 --> 00:39:16.660
But it also created a little space where you could store eggs or butter, cheese,

00:39:17.060 --> 00:39:19.300
a little bit of cold storage area in a hot climate.

00:39:19.520 --> 00:39:21.900
Well, it's in pretty good shape. Is it still in use?

00:39:22.240 --> 00:39:24.220
Oh, no. No, in the early 20th century,

00:39:24.220 --> 00:39:28.310
they discovered that the cisterns were breeding grounds for mosquitoes,

00:39:28.430 --> 00:39:29.970
which carried the yellow fever and the malaria.

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:34.640
So they were outlawed in New Orleans, but you still see them sometimes in the country around.

00:39:34.880 --> 00:39:39.560
Yeah, that's great. Now, you've shown me the public rooms. What about the private spaces?

00:39:39.860 --> 00:39:43.140
Well, they're all upstairs, and we can go right up the servants' stairway if you'd like to.

00:39:43.380 --> 00:39:43.800
Let's go.

00:39:49.760 --> 00:39:54.290
Now, Anne, you say servants, but isn't that really a euphemism for slaves?

00:39:54.990 --> 00:39:59.980
Well, not really. James used the word servants on his floor plan for the house, and I'll...

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:06.670
Although we know they were slaves from the 1860 census, we use James's word because we think he had a reason for that.

00:40:07.370 --> 00:40:09.050
But I'd like to show you one of the rooms.

00:40:09.570 --> 00:40:16.270
This belonged to Lorette, who was 42 years old in 1860 and probably took general charge of the whole household.

00:40:16.810 --> 00:40:18.750
This hardly looks like servants' quarters.

00:40:19.260 --> 00:40:21.210
The furniture is quite nice.

00:40:21.490 --> 00:40:22.310
By my standards, Lorette...

00:40:22.310 --> 00:40:23.710
Well, the rooms were very comfortable.

00:40:23.850 --> 00:40:27.630
A lot of visitors come here expecting to see pallets and chains and so forth.

00:40:27.770 --> 00:40:29.970
But in fact, these servants were almost part of...

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:35.790
of the family. And this furniture, however, to the Victorian eye, was really not very good.

00:40:35.990 --> 00:40:41.220
It was old, out of style, in many cases broken. And this was the kind of thing that we would

00:40:41.220 --> 00:40:43.720
put in our attic, but they sent back to the servants' room.

00:40:43.840 --> 00:40:44.800
So they're hand-me-downs.

00:40:45.040 --> 00:40:49.060
Yes, indeed. And very simple, just whitewashed walls and unfinished floors.

00:40:49.880 --> 00:40:52.360
Well, it looks as if Loretta just left here yesterday.

00:40:52.860 --> 00:40:56.920
Well, we try to make the house come alive, particularly when we do know something about

00:40:56.920 --> 00:40:59.560
a custom or something the servants might have done.

00:41:00.320 --> 00:41:04.170
Now, wouldn't this have been a great place to spend a sultry summer evening?

00:41:04.450 --> 00:41:08.050
I'm sure they spent many evenings out here, in fact, throughout the year.

00:41:08.330 --> 00:41:14.190
They would bring their tables and chairs, their sewing and reading and whatever outside to catch the cool breezes.

00:41:14.430 --> 00:41:17.270
Also, the gentlemen sometimes slept out there on hammocks.

00:41:17.760 --> 00:41:22.370
But this was probably the most exciting and innovative room in the house to the galliars.

00:41:22.700 --> 00:41:23.320
Look at this.

00:41:24.720 --> 00:41:29.960
It's a copper-soldered tub with pretty extraordinary...

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:58.160
casework here and spigots and a wooden commode with a china bowl. Yes, hand-painted china bowl. Look at that. Would the whole family, the whole household have used this? Well, certainly the Gallier children and Oglet and James and I'm sure their guests because they would probably like to see what a bathroom looked like. This was such a new idea, but the servants would have probably used the bathtub down in the kitchen. So it was a bit of a spectacle. Yes, it was.

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:06.360
This was a small room that was used either as a guest room when people came to town or as a sick room.

00:42:06.640 --> 00:42:12.640
It was the only room in the house that was painted, so it could be washed down after any illnesses with disinfectant.

00:42:12.870 --> 00:42:16.030
And also pink was recommended as a very good color for invalids.

00:42:16.130 --> 00:42:19.220
No kidding. Look at this.

00:42:19.360 --> 00:42:27.590
This could only have been a little girl's room with the little cakes and the milk pitchers and the brushes and everything.

00:42:27.930 --> 00:42:29.970
Well, the Galliers had four daughters that would have...

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:35.240
We all lived in this room, and we enjoy using these wonderful Victorian toys in a way that they might have.

00:42:35.260 --> 00:42:39.850
That's great. And this is very serious-looking mosquito netting here.

00:42:39.990 --> 00:42:43.010
Well, mosquitoes were a very serious problem in the 19th century,

00:42:43.010 --> 00:42:47.890
and this was absolutely essential throughout many different parts of the year, not just in the summertime.

00:42:48.150 --> 00:42:51.410
It would have been tucked in at night around the bed,

00:42:51.410 --> 00:42:56.390
and hopefully would have kept you free from the plague of the pesky insects, if they were called.

00:42:56.750 --> 00:42:59.330
This was James's library.

00:43:00.380 --> 00:43:03.600
Now wait, we're right over the main entrance here, where I came in.

00:43:03.620 --> 00:43:09.360
Indeed, this is the stairway here, and he designed the surrounding rooms with connecting doors

00:43:09.360 --> 00:43:13.990
so that they could be closed, and he could get a private area in here in which to work.

00:43:14.190 --> 00:43:20.190
He also installed the gas lighting, as well as the skylight, which provided both light and air to the space.

00:43:20.430 --> 00:43:23.370
So this was a clever and innovative way to use the area.

00:43:25.090 --> 00:43:26.670
This was the master bedroom.

00:43:27.750 --> 00:43:29.980
Look at this, this is an impo.

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:32.440
Posing an important-looking master bedroom, isn't it?

00:43:32.540 --> 00:43:34.380
Yes, isn't it? And the furniture as well.

00:43:35.460 --> 00:43:39.280
The furniture here was made in the cabinet shop of Proudhon-Millard,

00:43:39.870 --> 00:43:43.330
including the bed, the armoire, the nightstand, and several other pieces.

00:43:43.570 --> 00:43:48.270
And it gives you some idea of the fine craftsmanship that was available in New Orleans at the time.

00:43:48.670 --> 00:43:52.330
The woods all came from the islands, rosewood and mahogany, directly into the port.

00:43:52.550 --> 00:43:56.290
And then a wonderful cabinet-making area grew up here.

00:43:56.550 --> 00:43:59.950
Well, it's just what I would have expected from a man given to...

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:01.680
Claret and Havana's.

00:44:02.770 --> 00:44:03.490
Very elegant.

00:44:03.630 --> 00:44:05.270
You said you wanted to go out on the veranda,

00:44:05.450 --> 00:44:07.130
and this is the way to do it in these houses.

00:44:07.370 --> 00:44:08.070
Right through the window?

00:44:08.210 --> 00:44:08.870
Right through the window.

00:44:08.990 --> 00:44:09.990
They use them as doors.

00:44:10.750 --> 00:44:12.030
And look at this street scene.

00:44:12.780 --> 00:44:16.040
Now, where else in the whole world could you get a street scene like this?

00:44:16.160 --> 00:44:16.900
It's wonderful, isn't it?

00:44:17.060 --> 00:44:19.200
The Vicharay has so many different kinds of buildings.

00:44:19.520 --> 00:44:21.480
Fabulous texture, different building materials.

00:44:21.820 --> 00:44:22.580
Beautiful place.

00:44:23.040 --> 00:44:26.620
Well, I think the Galliard House is an extraordinary piece of Victoriana.

00:44:26.840 --> 00:44:29.900
I'd like to thank you and your colleagues for preserving it for all of us.

00:44:30.090 --> 00:44:31.390
Well, thank you for the compliment.

00:44:31.570 --> 00:44:34.870
We also think that it expresses well life in New Orleans at this time.

00:44:35.050 --> 00:44:36.330
Now, it is open to the public, right?

00:44:36.550 --> 00:44:37.050
Yes, indeed.

00:44:37.250 --> 00:44:41.470
We're open seven days a week, Monday through Saturday, 10 to 4, and also Sunday afternoon.

00:44:42.030 --> 00:44:45.610
And our last tour leaves at 4 o'clock, so you need to get here before that.

00:44:45.770 --> 00:44:47.190
And you will be leading the tours, I know?

00:44:47.390 --> 00:44:51.450
Sometimes, but we also have a wonderfully trained staff who will be glad to show you around.

00:44:51.670 --> 00:44:53.790
Well, Ann, thank you very much for showing it to us.

00:44:53.830 --> 00:44:55.070
Well, thank you all for coming.

00:44:55.720 --> 00:44:57.260
Now, how about the Café du Monde?

00:44:57.440 --> 00:44:58.280
Oh, great idea.

00:45:11.770 --> 00:45:15.020
Well, this is a budget job, which means no dumpster.

00:45:16.060 --> 00:45:19.900
So instead, our homeowner's dragged all of his construction debris out to the curb here.

00:45:19.900 --> 00:45:22.940
He's got it all stacked up, and the blue stuff's in bags.

00:45:23.420 --> 00:45:26.180
He's just hoping that the city will haul it away.

00:45:27.330 --> 00:45:29.390
Over here, though, is the good stuff.

00:45:30.250 --> 00:45:37.970
This laugh has been set aside because Elvis has a buddy who's going to haul this stuff away and actually make crab traps out of it.

00:45:38.030 --> 00:45:40.290
I guess that's something like our lobster pots up north.

00:45:43.170 --> 00:45:44.870
Hey, Steve.

00:45:45.090 --> 00:45:45.290
How you doing?

00:45:45.860 --> 00:45:47.940
You got a table saw and a jointer?

00:45:48.680 --> 00:45:50.840
Well, you know, we need the tools to do the job right.

00:45:50.980 --> 00:45:57.250
And so these tools will be used in more the finishing part of the job, and they've been loaned to us by a tool company.

00:45:57.330 --> 00:45:58.010
Boy, that's great.

00:45:58.910 --> 00:45:59.650
Boy, this room.

00:46:00.280 --> 00:46:05.130
the wall down and the beam up that's pretty nice proportions that makes a big difference and i think

00:46:05.130 --> 00:46:09.310
it's well worth opening it up yeah well i haven't been on the site for a couple of days so how about

00:46:09.310 --> 00:46:15.090
the nickel tour okay you remember on this side which is going to be the dining room we remove the

00:46:15.090 --> 00:46:20.520
wall here and footed the ceiling with a short beam which goes right into the chimney you got

00:46:20.520 --> 00:46:26.820
that all mortared in i see that's all set then we remove this lawn wall and replace that with

00:46:26.820 --> 00:46:29.980
another beam that goes from chimney to chimney, and that's all.

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:35.200
All wooded in also, and that's set, which really opens up that dining room area into the kitchen.

00:46:35.400 --> 00:46:36.300
Yeah, sure does.

00:46:37.220 --> 00:46:39.520
Then I've just started working on this angle wall.

00:46:40.040 --> 00:46:41.660
Now, this is going to be a pantry unit.

00:46:41.760 --> 00:46:45.850
There'll be a couple bifold doors that'll open up and shelves in here for storage,

00:46:45.870 --> 00:46:49.780
and then there'll be another bifold door which will open up to the electrical panel

00:46:49.780 --> 00:46:54.280
because there is no basement here, so all the circuit breakers will be contained in that area.

00:46:54.440 --> 00:46:54.560
Right.

00:46:55.210 --> 00:46:57.030
Now, the chimneys will all be replastered.

00:46:57.370 --> 00:46:59.970
Yeah, they'll put new... clean them up a little bit.

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:02.880
so the plaster will stick to them and just recoat all the brick.

00:47:03.360 --> 00:47:06.820
And this hall wall is framed up, and I notice it's blocked now, too.

00:47:07.140 --> 00:47:09.780
Yeah, Elvis, our homeowner, put the blocking in over the weekend.

00:47:09.920 --> 00:47:13.460
And, you know, the blocking is really needed because this is a 12-foot wall.

00:47:14.020 --> 00:47:17.280
And when you have studs that are that long, they tend to wave around,

00:47:17.340 --> 00:47:18.620
and they don't stay very straight.

00:47:19.000 --> 00:47:22.160
By running a row of blocking down the middle at the halfway point,

00:47:22.200 --> 00:47:23.560
it really stiffens up the wall.

00:47:23.720 --> 00:47:26.780
Great. So you've got your door framed in,

00:47:26.860 --> 00:47:29.370
but, boy, it looks like a huge framed opening.

00:47:29.430 --> 00:47:29.970
What's that all about?

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:33.490
Well, it is. All the doors that were in the house were going to recycle.

00:47:33.690 --> 00:47:38.540
And they're all three foot by about nine foot because they have these transom lights over them.

00:47:39.240 --> 00:47:42.720
And we really want to use those, especially in the hallways, in that hallway,

00:47:42.800 --> 00:47:45.120
because you want to bring as much light into it as you can.

00:47:45.240 --> 00:47:47.720
Sure. So what's the furniture arrangement in here?

00:47:48.020 --> 00:47:52.660
Okay, down at this end of the room, there'll be a twin bed across the wall this way

00:47:52.660 --> 00:47:56.270
with plenty of room for the dresser and a desk.

00:47:57.060 --> 00:47:59.960
Of course, this door has to stay the way the house was built.

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:04.400
and it's just not worth trying to take it out of here and patch the outside.

00:48:04.540 --> 00:48:05.720
We don't have the money to do that.

00:48:05.940 --> 00:48:08.820
But it'll let a lot of light in and ventilation, presumably.

00:48:09.040 --> 00:48:09.380
That's right.

00:48:10.250 --> 00:48:12.370
Then there's a closet in the middle, and it's a big one.

00:48:12.430 --> 00:48:16.050
It's nine feet long because it's going to be for the two girls that are going to be in this room.

00:48:16.740 --> 00:48:17.660
But why so short?

00:48:18.060 --> 00:48:20.020
Well, you know, in a lot of the houses around here,

00:48:20.040 --> 00:48:23.440
they've actually framed closets in like this but brought them right to the ceiling.

00:48:24.450 --> 00:48:28.290
And the problem with that is that if you want a storage area up there,

00:48:28.370 --> 00:48:29.970
you have to put doors, and that gets covered.

00:48:30.030 --> 00:48:34.260
complicated. You have to have a ladder to get up there. But the real problem is that

00:48:34.260 --> 00:48:38.950
these rooms are already taller than they are wide. So if you put a big wall up here where

00:48:38.950 --> 00:48:44.200
it's only six, seven feet wide, it makes it feel really tough. Yeah, yeah. So we'll keep

00:48:44.200 --> 00:48:48.400
it low, and they can still store things up there, and you could even put some plants

00:48:48.400 --> 00:48:52.770
on there. Good compromise. It also lets the light in from the windows. It doesn't block

00:48:52.770 --> 00:48:57.400
everything. Right. So what happens down here? Okay, now down here there'll be another bed

00:48:57.400 --> 00:48:58.780
on this wall.

00:48:58.900 --> 00:48:59.980
And again, plenty of room for us.

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:05.140
a dresser and a desk. Sort of a girl's suite. That's right. So directly across the hall I

00:49:05.140 --> 00:49:09.970
notice you've got this opening demoed and you're starting some framing here. That's right. There'll

00:49:09.970 --> 00:49:16.040
be a door opening here again with a transom light and this becomes their son's bedroom and

00:49:16.040 --> 00:49:22.180
this is a nice size room. And over here this door is framed in and furred out with what five-eighths

00:49:22.180 --> 00:49:27.500
plywood or something. Right. Well the reason we did that is because it's tough to patch in drywall.

00:49:27.500 --> 00:49:29.970
you know, a big opening like this, or the plaster, rather.

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:35.230
it's easier to bring everything out flush with the plaster and just drywall over the whole thing good

00:49:35.230 --> 00:49:40.880
so it'll save time in the long run that's right onward and we'll go down this hallway and another

00:49:40.880 --> 00:49:48.620
opening has been broken through into here and this will enter the master bedroom and on this side of

00:49:48.620 --> 00:49:54.380
the room there'll be a closet that runs all the way across again frame low because they want the

00:49:54.380 --> 00:49:59.880
open storage area above and this door will remain just like so where does the bed go

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:05.500
here somewhere? The bed will probably go right in this area, and then the room continues out

00:50:05.560 --> 00:50:11.790
all the way up to this wall, which still has to be demolished. There'll be a little wing wall

00:50:11.790 --> 00:50:17.420
on each side, and that'll open up into the master bathroom. So this is really a master bedroom suite.

00:50:17.660 --> 00:50:21.590
Right. Now, the ceiling has been blown out since the last time I was here.

00:50:22.070 --> 00:50:26.930
Well, we had an interesting discovery. The original house ended right here,

00:50:27.240 --> 00:50:29.980
right along this line here, and then it was added on to twice.

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:34.560
once for the kitchen and once for the bathroom and when they add it on for the

00:50:34.560 --> 00:50:39.220
kitchen they frame the roof along this angle right here and then when they

00:50:39.220 --> 00:50:43.400
added on the bathroom I guess they decided to frame a new roof and up above

00:50:43.400 --> 00:50:49.580
that span over both of the rooms so seeing that we decided it would be better

00:50:49.580 --> 00:50:53.920
